In the example shown in Figure G.1:
— the primary datum consists of a straight line;
— the secondary datum consists of a plane;
— the tertiary datum consists of a plane.
The datum system consists of a set of a plane, a straight line (on the plane) and a point (on the line). The
sequence of the datums determines the situation features:
— the straight line is defined by the primary datum;
— the plane includes the straight line and is orientated from the secondary datum;
— the point is the intersection between the straight line and the tertiary datum.

The resulting datum system (i.e. set of one or more situation features, see Figure I.3) consists of the
following:
— a plane (corresponding to the primary datum A);
— a point (intersection between the primary datum A and the secondary datum B);
— a straight line (parallel to the tertiary datum C, included in the plane and through with the secondary
datum B).
